// World generation shape. Edit and save: the running app reloads this file and
// regenerates the world on the current seed, so a value can be judged by eye
// in a couple of seconds. F6 rolls a new seed, F7 cycles preset, F8 steps back
// to the previous seed.
//
// Every field is documented on `WorldgenParams` in `src/worldgen/params.rs`.
// Lengths are in cells; wavelengths are in cells of world x.
//
// Nothing writes this file back — unlike the material `.ron` files, there is
// no panel save path here, so these comments are safe.
//
// Presets are written out in full rather than inheriting, so that every value
// in play is visible at the point of tuning. A preset that omits a field still
// loads (it falls back to the `rolling` values compiled into
// `WorldgenParams::default`), which is what keeps adding a field later from
// breaking this file. `rolling` and that fallback are asserted equal by
// `the_default_preset_matches_the_compiled_in_fallback`.
//
// Two numbers are load-bearing rather than decorative, and are worth
// understanding before sweeping them:
//
//   soil_slope_cutoff  Soil is omitted from ground steeper than this fraction
//                      of its own angle of repose. It is the whole at-rest
//                      guarantee for powder — raise it past 1.0 and generated
//                      soil will avalanche the moment the world loads.
//   sky_rows           How much air the composition gets. Too little and the
//                      frame is all rock; the surface wants room above it.
(
    default: "rolling",
    presets: {
        // The reference world: a ridge, a valley, and bluffs in patches.
        // What every other preset is a variation on.
        "rolling": (
            sky_rows: 190.0,
            relief_amplitude: 46.0,
            massif_amplitude: 260.0,
            massif_wavelength: 2600.0,
            hill_amplitude: 30.0,
            hill_wavelength: 150.0,
            detail_amplitude: 2.5,
            detail_wavelength: 28.0,
            warp_strength: 34.0,
            warp_wavelength: 130.0,
            terrace_step: 26.0,
            terrace_strength: 0.9,
            riser_roughness: 0.45,
            mask_wavelength: 150.0,
            terrace_slope_lo: 0.6,
            terrace_slope_hi: 2.0,
            soil_depth: 105.0,
            soil_slope_cutoff: 0.8,
            bedrock_band: 4.0,
            strata_thickness: 9.0,
            strata_tilt: 0.06,
            strata_fold: 6.0,
            pocket_density: 0.6,
            lens_roughness: 1.0,
            talus_max_height: 12.0,
            residual_density: 1.4,
            spring_flow: 5.0,
            brow_chance: 0.8,
            table_damping: 0.35,
            table_offset: 12.0,
            capillary_fringe: 24.0,
            pond_min_depth: 2.0,
            pond_min_width: 4.0,
            aridity: 0.35,
            dune_amplitude: 14.0,
            dune_wavelength: 46.0,
            dune_variation: 0.7,
            aridity_table_drop: 90.0,
            region_variation: 0.75,
            palette_field: 0.30,
            vault_density: 1.6,
            vault_min_depth: 200,
            vault_bedrock_margin: 16,
            world_age: 0.8,
            moss_density: 0.10,
            tree_density: 0.26,
            grass_density: 0.50,
            life_cluster_wavelength: 70.0,
        ),
        // **The held world's country** (`cargo run --release --bin druid`,
        // `Reports/held-world-game-concept-2026-09-13.md`). A land for
        // *growing things in*, which is close to the inverse of every preset
        // above: those exist so you can tunnel into rock, and that game has
        // no mining, no caving and no destruction to spend it on.
        //
        // **Expressed as preset data rather than as a `Spec` variant, and
        // that was a deliberate choice.** A new enum arm would need a match
        // in `generate_ablated` and `generate_reported_with`, and
        // `worldgencheck.sh` gates *presets* -- it sweeps every preset at the
        // shipped size -- so a `Spec` would have been the one route that is
        // NOT checked for a pass eating another's output.
        //
        // **Owner's verdict on the first version of this preset, 2026-09-13:**
        // *"Revamp the world gen. This looks too much like The gnome game. I
        // don't like it. Number one issue is It should be way more soil, way
        // less rock"*, and on the render: *"The camera is ok, but it should be
        // mostly soil, little to no rock"*. Measured over 8 player viewports x
        // 3 seeds at 2560x960 (`world_look mode=composition world=2560x960`),
        // the first version was **79% rock / 10% loose**, with a median of
        // **22 cells** of ground above the first rock. It now reads **36%
        // rock / 54% loose** at **115 cells**, and soil is the single largest
        // thing on screen at 54% against the whole rock vocabulary's 36%.
        //
        // **The lever everyone reaches for is the one that does nothing, and
        // that is the finding worth keeping.** `soil_depth` is not what limits
        // how deep the ground is: `column::plan_from` gives no soil at all to
        // any column steeper than `soil_slope_cutoff * repose`, and
        // `column::taper_cover` then walks that zero outward one repose step
        // (~0.7 cells) per column, so **one crag thins the blanket for a
        // couple of hundred columns either side of it**. Measured: raising
        // `soil_depth` from 210 to **1,000** -- 4.8x -- moved the viewport
        // soil share from 8.4% to **8.5%**. What moves it is removing the
        // steep columns, and the two that produce them are `world_age` and
        // the short-wavelength terrain terms.
        //
        // The changes from the first version, grouped by what they are for:
        //
        //   world_age      0.8 -> 0    **the single biggest one.** Erosion is
        //                              what cuts the gullies and leaves the
        //                              hard bands standing, and every one of
        //                              those is a bare column. Measured the
        //                              wrong way round first: *more* erosion
        //                              made it worse, `world_age` 1.0 -> 8.0
        //                              taking bare columns 7.3% -> 12.7%. As
        //                              `flat` already does, 0 is a guaranteed
        //                              no-op rather than a small age, and it
        //                              is legible in the fiction too -- this
        //                              land has no history of being cut up.
        //   hill_amp/wavelength,       the short-wavelength steepness. A hill
        //   detail_amp/wavelength,     term of amplitude 30 at wavelength 150
        //   warp_strength/wavelength,  peaks at a slope of 1.26 against a soil
        //   terrace_strength 0.35 -> 0 cutoff of 0.71, so hills alone were
        //                              stripping the blanket; the warp then
        //                              multiplies whatever slope it lands on.
        //                              Long wavelengths are safe -- a 170-cell
        //                              massif over 3,200 peaks at 0.24 -- so
        //                              the *shape* moves down into
        //                              `relief_amplitude` and the massif
        //                              rather than being flattened away.
        //   relief_amplitude 34 -> 48  ...which is that move. Broad swells a
        //   massif_amplitude 70 -> 170 walk crosses, in place of hills a walk
        //   massif_wavelength -> 3200  climbs. The 70 was set to kill the
        //                              screen-high walls and it did that by
        //                              removing the landscape as well.
        //   soil_depth     210 -> 380  now that it binds again. Not higher:
        //                              `passes::soil_shade` ramps the profile
        //                              over the blanket's *own* depth, so a
        //                              600-deep blanket puts the whole visible
        //                              cutaway in the single darkest tone and
        //                              the ground reads as one flat slab. 380
        //                              costs ~3 points of rock against 600 and
        //                              keeps two horizons in view.
        //   region_variation .75 -> .3 `sediment` scales `soil_depth` per
        //                              region, and at 0.75 it swings 4x -- so
        //                              whole regions came out thin.
        //   talus_max_height -> 0      grey gravel aprons, and they land at
        //   brow_chance      -> 0      the *top* of the profile. Brows and
        //   residual_density -> 0      tors are rock in the air. All three are
        //                              landforms for a game with a pick in it.
        //   strata_thickness 9 -> 34   what is left of the rock stops wearing
        //   strata_tilt   .06 -> .02   the sandbox's stripes. The banding is
        //   strata_fold      6 -> 2    the thing that reads as the other game,
        //                              so where a massif crest does break the
        //                              surface it stands as one quiet mass.
        //
        //   warp_strength  12 -> 0     **the at-rest fix, and it costs no
        //   soil_slope_cutoff          soil at all.** Domain warp displaces
        //                1.1 -> 1.0    the sample position, so its own
        //                              gradient *multiplies* the slope of
        //                              every term downstream of it and can
        //                              push a column past repose that no
        //                              amplitude here would. With the ground
        //                              this gentle it was the only thing
        //                              still doing that: turning it off moved
        //                              the rock share by 0.2 points and took
        //                              `generated_terrain_is_already_at_rest`
        //                              from one soil grain in motion to none.
        //
        // **`soil_slope_cutoff` goes DOWN, to the documented limit.** It is
        // the whole at-rest guarantee for powder -- this file's header says
        // soil above 1.0 avalanches -- and the held world's first version
        // shipped **1.1**, past it. That is not academic: at 1.1 with the warp
        // already off, the at-rest guard fails on **seed 12**. It passed only
        // because the guard sweeps **five** seeds, which CLAUDE.md's own
        // "six seeds is not a sweep" rule says is a sample. Widened to twenty
        // locally, `warp 0 / cutoff 1.0` is the only combination tried that
        // holds -- `warp 4 / cutoff 1.0` fails on seed 14. Raising the cutoff
        // is the strongest lever here by far (5.0 reaches 9.7% rock) and it is
        // exactly the one that must not be pulled: it buys soil by placing it
        // on ground steeper than it can stand on. Every number above is bought
        // by making the *ground* gentle enough that a safe gate stops firing.
        // The 3-point rock cost of 1.1 -> 1.0 is the price of that, and it is
        // the right trade in a game where nothing is supposed to move.
        //
        // Unchanged from the first version, and still right: no vaults and no
        // pockets (nothing digs), the wetter water table, and the raised life
        // densities -- this world is judged on what grows in it.
        "druid": (
            sky_rows: 190.0,
            relief_amplitude: 48.0,
            massif_amplitude: 170.0,
            massif_wavelength: 3200.0,
            hill_amplitude: 14.0,
            hill_wavelength: 420.0,
            detail_amplitude: 1.1,
            detail_wavelength: 40.0,
            warp_strength: 0.0,
            warp_wavelength: 240.0,
            terrace_step: 26.0,
            terrace_strength: 0.0,
            riser_roughness: 0.45,
            mask_wavelength: 150.0,
            terrace_slope_lo: 0.6,
            terrace_slope_hi: 2.0,
            soil_depth: 380.0,
            soil_slope_cutoff: 1.0,
            bedrock_band: 4.0,
            strata_thickness: 34.0,
            strata_tilt: 0.02,
            strata_fold: 2.0,
            pocket_density: 0.0,
            lens_roughness: 1.0,
            talus_max_height: 0.0,
            residual_density: 0.0,
            spring_flow: 7.0,
            brow_chance: 0.0,
            table_damping: 0.35,
            table_offset: 6.0,
            capillary_fringe: 36.0,
            pond_min_depth: 1.5,
            pond_min_width: 3.0,
            aridity: 0.15,
            dune_amplitude: 14.0,
            dune_wavelength: 46.0,
            dune_variation: 0.7,
            aridity_table_drop: 90.0,
            region_variation: 0.30,
            palette_field: 0.30,
            vault_density: 0.0,
            vault_min_depth: 200,
            vault_bedrock_margin: 16,
            world_age: 0.0,
            moss_density: 0.18,
            tree_density: 0.50,
            grass_density: 0.72,
            life_cluster_wavelength: 70.0,
        ),
        // Benched country: bluffs over most of the relief instead of in
        // patches. The preset for judging whether terracing reads as geology
        // or as a flight of stairs — if it ever reads as stairs, it will read
        // as stairs here first.
        "terraced": (
            sky_rows: 190.0,
            relief_amplitude: 40.0,
            massif_amplitude: 230.0,
            massif_wavelength: 2400.0,
            hill_amplitude: 26.0,
            hill_wavelength: 170.0,
            detail_amplitude: 2.5,
            detail_wavelength: 28.0,
            warp_strength: 30.0,
            warp_wavelength: 130.0,
            terrace_step: 20.0,
            terrace_strength: 0.95,
            riser_roughness: 0.45,
            mask_wavelength: 110.0,
            terrace_slope_lo: 0.6,
            terrace_slope_hi: 2.0,
            soil_depth: 88.0,
            soil_slope_cutoff: 0.8,
            bedrock_band: 4.0,
            strata_thickness: 10.0,
            strata_tilt: 0.05,
            strata_fold: 7.0,
            pocket_density: 0.6,
            lens_roughness: 1.0,
            talus_max_height: 14.0,
            residual_density: 1.4,
            spring_flow: 5.0,
            brow_chance: 0.9,
            table_damping: 0.35,
            table_offset: 26.0,
            capillary_fringe: 24.0,
            pond_min_depth: 2.0,
            pond_min_width: 4.0,
            aridity: 0.3,
            dune_amplitude: 12.0,
            dune_wavelength: 46.0,
            dune_variation: 0.7,
            aridity_table_drop: 90.0,
            region_variation: 0.7,
            palette_field: 0.30,
            vault_density: 1.6,
            vault_min_depth: 200,
            vault_bedrock_margin: 16,
            world_age: 0.8,
            moss_density: 0.10,
            tree_density: 0.22,
            grass_density: 0.50,
            life_cluster_wavelength: 70.0,
        ),
        // Deep relief, thin soil, tall scree, water far down. Big vertical
        // faces to strike, mine and stand under.
        "canyon": (
            sky_rows: 160.0,
            relief_amplitude: 70.0,
            massif_amplitude: 380.0,
            massif_wavelength: 3000.0,
            hill_amplitude: 18.0,
            hill_wavelength: 200.0,
            detail_amplitude: 3.0,
            detail_wavelength: 26.0,
            warp_strength: 40.0,
            warp_wavelength: 150.0,
            terrace_step: 34.0,
            terrace_strength: 0.95,
            riser_roughness: 0.5,
            mask_wavelength: 170.0,
            terrace_slope_lo: 0.6,
            terrace_slope_hi: 2.0,
            soil_depth: 48.0,
            soil_slope_cutoff: 0.7,
            bedrock_band: 5.0,
            strata_thickness: 12.0,
            strata_tilt: 0.09,
            strata_fold: 8.0,
            pocket_density: 0.8,
            lens_roughness: 1.0,
            talus_max_height: 18.0,
            residual_density: 1.4,
            spring_flow: 5.0,
            brow_chance: 0.9,
            table_damping: 0.3,
            table_offset: 70.0,
            capillary_fringe: 20.0,
            pond_min_depth: 2.0,
            pond_min_width: 4.0,
            aridity: 0.55,
            dune_amplitude: 16.0,
            dune_wavelength: 52.0,
            dune_variation: 0.75,
            aridity_table_drop: 80.0,
            region_variation: 0.85,
            palette_field: 0.45,
            vault_density: 1.6,
            vault_min_depth: 200,
            vault_bedrock_margin: 16,
            world_age: 1.0,
            moss_density: 0.06,
            tree_density: 0.10,
            grass_density: 0.31,
            life_cluster_wavelength: 70.0,
        ),
        // Low relief, deep soil, high water table, dense life. The wettest
        // world the parameters allow — the upper bound when judging whether
        // water helps the place or drowns it.
        "wetland": (
            sky_rows: 220.0,
            relief_amplitude: 24.0,
            massif_amplitude: 120.0,
            massif_wavelength: 2200.0,
            hill_amplitude: 16.0,
            hill_wavelength: 200.0,
            detail_amplitude: 2.0,
            detail_wavelength: 34.0,
            warp_strength: 24.0,
            warp_wavelength: 140.0,
            terrace_step: 26.0,
            terrace_strength: 0.3,
            riser_roughness: 0.35,
            mask_wavelength: 180.0,
            terrace_slope_lo: 0.6,
            terrace_slope_hi: 2.0,
            soil_depth: 135.0,
            soil_slope_cutoff: 0.85,
            bedrock_band: 3.0,
            strata_thickness: 8.0,
            strata_tilt: 0.03,
            strata_fold: 5.0,
            pocket_density: 0.5,
            lens_roughness: 1.0,
            talus_max_height: 8.0,
            residual_density: 1.4,
            spring_flow: 5.0,
            brow_chance: 0.5,
            table_damping: 0.35,
            table_offset: 8.0,
            capillary_fringe: 30.0,
            pond_min_depth: 2.0,
            pond_min_width: 4.0,
            aridity: 0.08,
            dune_amplitude: 10.0,
            dune_wavelength: 46.0,
            dune_variation: 0.6,
            aridity_table_drop: 70.0,
            region_variation: 0.45,
            palette_field: 0.15,
            vault_density: 1.6,
            vault_min_depth: 200,
            vault_bedrock_margin: 16,
            world_age: 0.8,
            moss_density: 0.25,
            tree_density: 0.45,
            grass_density: 0.63,
            life_cluster_wavelength: 70.0,
        ),
        // No water anywhere: the table is pushed below the world floor, so no
        // pond is generated and the moisture floor stays zero. This is the
        // pivot lever made concrete — if the water table turns out not to be
        // fun, this preset is what the world looks like without it, one press
        // of F7 away.
        //
        // `table_offset` here is a **sentinel, not a depth**: it has to land
        // past `app::WORLD_HEIGHT` or the lever is not a lever. At 400 it
        // stopped being one when the world grew — `moisture_init` writes a
        // moisture floor for every row at or below `table_y`, so a 2560-row
        // world put a damp table at row ~570 and left two million cells of
        // wet rock in the world that is supposed to have none. 4000 clears a
        // world four times taller again; `the_dry_presets_keep_their_table_
        // below_the_world_floor` asserts it against `WORLD_HEIGHT` rather
        // than against a literal, so the next size change fails a test
        // instead of quietly wetting the desert.
        "arid": (
            sky_rows: 190.0,
            relief_amplitude: 18.0,
            massif_amplitude: 170.0,
            massif_wavelength: 2800.0,
            hill_amplitude: 9.0,
            hill_wavelength: 150.0,
            detail_amplitude: 3.0,
            detail_wavelength: 24.0,
            warp_strength: 34.0,
            warp_wavelength: 130.0,
            terrace_step: 26.0,
            terrace_strength: 0.5,
            riser_roughness: 0.4,
            mask_wavelength: 150.0,
            terrace_slope_lo: 0.6,
            terrace_slope_hi: 2.0,
            soil_depth: 80.0,
            soil_slope_cutoff: 0.75,
            bedrock_band: 4.0,
            strata_thickness: 9.0,
            strata_tilt: 0.07,
            strata_fold: 6.0,
            pocket_density: 0.9,
            lens_roughness: 1.0,
            talus_max_height: 16.0,
            residual_density: 1.4,
            spring_flow: 0.0,
            brow_chance: 0.85,
            table_damping: 0.35,
            table_offset: 4000.0,
            capillary_fringe: 24.0,
            pond_min_depth: 2.0,
            pond_min_width: 4.0,
            aridity: 0.92,
            dune_amplitude: 18.0,
            dune_wavelength: 58.0,
            dune_variation: 0.85,
            aridity_table_drop: 60.0,
            region_variation: 0.5,
            palette_field: 0.15,
            vault_density: 1.6,
            vault_min_depth: 200,
            vault_bedrock_margin: 16,
            world_age: 0.7,
            moss_density: 0.0,
            tree_density: 0.0,
            grass_density: 0.0,
            life_cluster_wavelength: 70.0,
        ),
        // **The structural test bed.** Dead flat bare rock under a tall sky:
        // the world for judging whether something a player *built* stands,
        // as opposed to whether the world itself does.
        //
        // It is a preset rather than a code path on purpose. The question
        // "does this room hold" needs a ground with no slope, no soil to
        // sink into, no talus to prop things up and no trees in the way --
        // and every one of those is already a number here, so a whole
        // parallel "test world" mode would be a second way to build a world
        // that could drift out of step with the real one. F7 reaches it.
        //
        // Every amplitude is zero and every wavelength is left alone: a
        // wavelength is a divisor in places, and zeroing the thing that
        // multiplies is what makes the surface flat without asking whether
        // any of them is load-bearing.
        //
        //   sky_rows 200   Room for the 200x160 reference room `B` stamps,
        //                  with margin. At the shipped 95 there is not
        //                  enough air and `B` correctly refuses.
        //   soil_depth 0   Bare stone. Building on sand is a different
        //                  structural question -- `GRANULAR_CAPACITY_DIVISOR`
        //                  cuts a cell's capacity by 64x when what is under
        //                  it is loose grain -- and mixing the two is how a
        //                  test bed stops telling you which one you measured.
        //   table_offset   Past the world floor, as `arid` does, so no pond
        //                  fills the room while you are watching it -- and
        //                  so no moisture floor is written into the rock
        //                  either, which is the half that broke when the
        //                  world grew. See `arid` above for the sentinel.
        "flat": (
            sky_rows: 200.0,
            relief_amplitude: 0.0,
            massif_amplitude: 0.0,
            massif_wavelength: 2600.0,
            hill_amplitude: 0.0,
            hill_wavelength: 150.0,
            detail_amplitude: 0.0,
            detail_wavelength: 28.0,
            warp_strength: 0.0,
            warp_wavelength: 130.0,
            terrace_step: 26.0,
            terrace_strength: 0.0,
            riser_roughness: 0.0,
            mask_wavelength: 150.0,
            terrace_slope_lo: 0.6,
            terrace_slope_hi: 2.0,
            soil_depth: 0.0,
            soil_slope_cutoff: 0.8,
            bedrock_band: 4.0,
            strata_thickness: 9.0,
            strata_tilt: 0.0,
            strata_fold: 0.0,
            pocket_density: 0.0,
            lens_roughness: 1.0,
            talus_max_height: 0.0,
            brow_chance: 0.0,
            table_damping: 0.35,
            table_offset: 4000.0,
            capillary_fringe: 24.0,
            pond_min_depth: 2.0,
            pond_min_width: 4.0,
            aridity: 0.0,
            dune_amplitude: 0.0,
            dune_wavelength: 46.0,
            dune_variation: 0.0,
            aridity_table_drop: 0.0,
            region_variation: 0.0,
            palette_field: 0.00,
            // The structural test bed: no chambers, so the destruction workstream's
            // control renders stay byte-identical. See `region_variation` above.
            vault_density: 0.0,
            vault_min_depth: 200,
            vault_bedrock_margin: 16,
            // Same reasoning as `vault_density`: `region_variation` being 0
            // does *not* zero `Character::formation` on its own -- the
            // single global region it falls back to is `neutral()`, whose
            // `formation` is `1.0`, not `0.0` -- so this has to be spelled
            // out explicitly or the "dead-level bare rock" bed grows tors.
            residual_density: 0.0,
            spring_flow: 0.0,
            // Dead-level bare rock stays dead-level: erosion is a plan-space
            // history the structural test bed must not have, or its control
            // renders drift out from under the destruction workstream. Every
            // other preset carries a nonzero age (round-4 task 4); this is
            // the deliberate exception, not an oversight.
            world_age: 0.0,
            moss_density: 0.0,
            tree_density: 0.0,
            grass_density: 0.0,
            life_cluster_wavelength: 70.0,
        ),
    },
)
